OK, some of you don't want to read docs (even when there's a whole
directory full of them). So here's the brief version of how to make
MiNT run. For a list of new features in this version of MiNT, see the
file "readme.new".

(1) put mint.prg in the AUTO folder of your boot disk. Make sure it's the
last thing in the folder -- anything after MiNT won't run. TT users:
this may not work for you; see below.

(2) put init.prg, gem.prg, and bgacc.acc in the root directory of your boot
disk

(3) reboot

(4) MiNT should run and a prompt ("% ") will appear. Type "exit" (without
the quotes) and press return to continue booting without MiNT. Type "gem"
and press return to get the desktop, running under MiNT.

(5) If you chose to boot with MiNT, a desk accessory entry labelled
"Background Prg" should appear. Choosing this for the first time will
bring up a file selector box. Use the file selector to choose a TOS
or TTP program to run. You can return to the desktop by pressing
the Control key and the "0" key on the number pad at the same time;
the TOS/TTP program will continue running, and to re-connect to it
select "Background Prg" again from the DA menu. See bgacc.doc for
more information, and for important information about bugs.

(6) If you have a TT, the above sequence may not work for you; for some
reason, "gem.prg" crashes the system. You may, instead, run MiNT from
the desktop or install it as an auto booting application. If you want
to run GEM applications under MiNT, you'll have to run them from
a CLI; thus, MiNT should be installed as a GEM application so that
everything will be properly set up for the CLI.

(7) If you have TOS 1.2 or higher, you can install some new drives on
the desktop while running MiNT. Drive V: shows all of the BIOS devices
available under MiNT, and drive X: shows all the processes active at the
present time. You're allowed to rename processes just as though they were
files. You can also delete them by dragging them to the desktop; you should
be careful to make sure you really want them deleted, though! Deleting the
MiNT process is impossible; deleting INIT or BGACC is possible, but is
a very, very bad idea.

(8) While MiNT is running, there are some special keys available:

CTRL-ALT-C: kills all processes that are reading from/writing to the
	screen. Useful for getting out of hung programs.
CTRL-ALT-\ or CTRL-ALT-UNDO: similar to CTRL-ALT-C, but somewhat more
	fatal (fewer programs choose to catch the signal these keys
	raise)
CTRL-ALT-DEL: resets the computer, as in TOS 1.4 and higher
CTRL-ALT-F1: raises the "debug level" of MiNT. Pressing this key once
	causes a message to be printed whenever an error occurs;
	pressing it twice causes a detailed trace of system calls.
	This key can be *very*, *very* useful in tracking down problems
	with any program!
CTRL-ALT-F2: lowers the debugging level -- press it as many times as
	you pressed CTRL-ALT-F1 to turn off all debugging
CTRL-ALT-F3: toggles debugging output between the screen (default) and
	the printer
CTRL-ALT-F5: shows all memory being used in the system
CTRL-ALT-F6: shows all active processes in the system

CTRL-Z: suspends processes reading from/writing to the screen if the
	keyboard is not in "raw" mode. Use this key only if you're
	running a shell that, like you, understands job control.
CTRL-ALT-Z: suspends processes reading from/writing to the screen
	no matter what. The warning with CTRL-ALT-Z applies doubly here!

(8) If you have Minix partitions on your (fixed) hard drive, they can
be accessed via MiNT just as though they were TOS partitions. See
"minixfs.doc" for more details. The "minit" program that comes in
mntutl8b.zoo may be used to create Minix partitions, but I recommend
that you know what you're doing before trying this!


Other Docs:

"readme" contains a general introduction to MiNT
"readme.new" summarizes the new features of this version of MiNT
"copying" says under what conditions you can make copies of MiNT
"mint.doc" contains more detailed information about MiNT, including
   information of interest to programmers
"bgacc.doc" contains information about the background program accessory
"init.doc" tells about the default shell that runs when MiNT starts up;
   you can use this shell to do lots of things
"features" is a list of areas where MiNT is deliberately incompatible with
   TOS
"bugs" is a list of known bugs in MiNT -- read this one! Also, remember that
   there are lots of unknown bugs in MiNT as well.
"problems" is an (incomplete) list of programs that are known to have
   problems working under MiNT
"minixfs.doc" is a description of the (experimental) Minix filesystem
   code by Stephen N. Henson.

